module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Records

let content(): String =
  """# Records

Records group related values into named fields. Each field can have a different type.
Separate fields with commas or write each field on its own line.

## Define

Define a record type by specifying the name and type of each field.

  type Person = { name: String, age: Int64 }

Record fields can hold other records, such as `resident: Person`.
A record type can take a type parameter, as in `type Box<'a> = { item: 'a }`.

Record types with the same field names and types in the same order are treated
as the same type, even if they have different type names.

## Construct
Write the type name followed by its fields and their values in braces.
All fields are required, and each value must match its declared type.
You can write the fields in any order.

  let person = Person { name = "Jo", age = 30 }

You can also separate fields with newlines instead of commas.

  Person
    { name = "Jo"
      age = 30 }

## Access

Access a field using dot notation.

  person.name

## Update

Use `with` to update one or more fields in a record. This creates a new record
with the updated values and keeps all other field values the same.
The original record is unchanged.

  { person with age = 31 }   // Person { name = "Jo", age = 31 }
  { person with age = 31, name = "Jane" }   // Person { name = "Jane", age = 31 }

## Pattern matching

Record patterns match only the fields you specify. Use `{ name }` as shorthand
for `{ name = name }` to bind a field to a variable with the same name.

  match person with
  | { age = 0 } -> "newborn"
  | { name } -> name

See `dark docs pattern-matching` for more examples.

## Equality

Two records of the same type are equal if all their field values are equal."""
